[recv] {"method":"item/completed","params":{"item":{"type":"agentMessage","id":"msg_0728125b284fe4c1016a2078bb7788819787d01990b08ea481","text":"Implemented a minimal RP2040 USB breakout in [index.circuit.tsx](/workspace/index.circuit.tsx). It includes:\n\n- JLCPCB-imported RP2040 and 3.3 V regulator\n- Built-in USB-C connector, no registry components\n- RP2040 support parts: QSPI flash model, 12 MHz crystal, decoupling, CC resistors\n- Green status LED on GPIO25\n- BOOT and RUN pads\n- Two 8-pin breakout headers with GPIO, SWD, RUN, 3V3, and GND\n- No `routingDisabled`\n\nValidation run:\n\n- `npm run typecheck`: passed\n- `tsci check netlist`: 0 errors, 0 warnings\n- `tsci check placement`: 0 errors, 0 warnings\n- `tsci check schematic-placement`: no overlaps; remaining output is schematic style suggestions\n- `tsci build`: completed successfully and wrote `dist/index/circuit.json`; it still prints non-fatal tscircuit metadata warnings about USB-C manufacturer part number, generic pin attributes, and inferred supplier footprint matching for built-in passives\n\n**TSCircuit Developer Feedback**\n- Confusing/surprising: `tsci build` exits successfully while printing many scary “missing trace” and metadata warnings; it is hard to tell which are fatal versus advisory.\n- Most time wasted: autorouter behavior around a dense imported JLCPCB flash footprint and a too-large breakout header set.\n- Helpful changes: clearer severity labels in build output, better default USB-C manufacturer-part handling, and docs showing valid `testpoint` footprint props.\n- Specific friction: `tsci check trace-length` requires a net argument, `routing-difficulty` stalled until manually killed, and React `key` behavior on generated JSX traces produced misleading warnings.","phase":"final_answer","memoryCitation":null},"threadId":"019e8ecc-76ea-7a23-a38a-5e7d3a9e9ac2","turnId":"019e8ecc-7701-7a81-a475-1d589a425a6e","completedAtMs":1780512962464}}
